  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\n<img width=\"949\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/efccffee-6793-49fe-bb98-be07bce4bfc7\" />\n\n**Proposal**\n\nWould it be possible to do one of the following:\n\n1. Document this behavior in [the form element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#the-form-element) specification.\n2. Chime in on whether this should be [considered a bug](https://issues.chromium.org/issues/410825518).\n\nAs a developer, I heavily rely on the specifications to drive the code I write. In this case — I just ended up being caught off guard.\n\n**Context**\n\nI encountered this issue when trying to create form-associated custom element which acts as a submit button (note this tangentially-related [thread](https://github.com/WICG/webcomponents/issues/814)). I basically wanted to write a line of code like `const onClick = () => this.form.submit();`. However, that _only works_ if `this.id !== 'submit'`. If-and-only-if either of the reset / submit buttons have exactly the id of “reset” / “submit” does this _shadowing_ of the prototype method occur.\n\n**Workaround**\n\nMy current workaround is to instead write a line like `const onClick = () => HTMLFormElement.prototype.submit.call(this.form)`. That works, but I’d be surprised if that was the intention of the specification.\n\n**Example Document**\n\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n    <title>form element &ldquo;submit&rdquo; shadow</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <form id=\"form\" style=\"width: 600px;\">\n      <p>\n        Clicking on &ldquo;Submit&rdquo; will print out the following properties\n        on the form:\n      </p>\n      <ul>\n        <li><code>form.submit</code></li>\n        <li><code>form.__proto__.submit</code></li>\n        <li><code>form.reset</code></li>\n        <li><code>form.__proto__.reset</code></li>\n      </ul>\n      <p>\n        Note that the <code>form.submit</code> property is a reference to the\n        <code>&lt;input id=&quot;submit&quot; type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;</code>\n        element &mdash; seemingly because the &ldquo;id&rdquo; attribute is set\n        to <em>exactly</em> &ldquo;submit&rdquo;. This appears to\n        <em>shadow</em> the prototype method of the same name.\n      </p>\n      <p>\n        By contrast, the <code>form.submit</code> property does not appear to\n        shadow the prototype method &mdash; i.e., because the &ldquo;id&rdquo;\n        attribute is <em>not</em> &ldquo;reset&rdquo;.\n      </p>\n      <p>\n        I looked at the\n        <a\n          href=\"https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/forms.html#the-form-element\"\n          target=\"_blank\">\n          form specification</a>,\n        but did not see any references to such shadowy behavior. Is it meant to\n        work this way?\n      </p>\n      <section>\n        <input id=\"input\" type=\"text\" name=\"input\">\n        <input id=\"submit\" type=\"submit\">\n        <input id=\"atypical-reset-identifier\" type=\"reset\">\n      </section>\n      <output id=\"output\"></output>\n    </form>\n    <script>\n      const form = document.getElementById('form');\n      const output = document.getElementById('output');\n      form.addEventListener('submit', event => {\n        event.preventDefault();\n        output.setHTMLUnsafe(`\n          <ul>\n            <li><code>form.submit:           ${form.submit}</code></li>\n            <li><code>form.__proto__.submit: ${form.__proto__.submit}</code></li>\n            <li><code>form.reset:            ${form.reset}</code></li>\n            <li><code>form.__proto__.reset:  ${form.__proto__.reset}</code></li>\n          </ul>\n        `);\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nAnd of course — thank you so much for taking the time to consider this ❤",
